3. Mathematical Foundations

3.1 The State-Space as a Discrete Metric Space

Let the integers \mathbb{Z} be equipped with the standard discrete metric

d(x,y) = |x - y|.

The pair (\mathbb{Z}, d) forms a metric space. A counting sequence starting at s_0 \in \mathbb{Z} is a finite sequence S = (s_0, s_1, \dots, s_m) such that for each i,

|s_{i+1} - s_i| = 1.

The sequence represents a walk on the graph G = (\mathbb{Z}, E) with edges between consecutive integers.

A geodesic from a to b is a path of minimum length. In this space, any path where each step strictly decreases the distance to b and eventually reaches b is a geodesic. For our prompt, s_0 = 11 and \tau = 10. The geodesic has length d(11,10)=1 and the unique geodesic (up to trivial revisiting) is S_{\text{geo}} = (11, 10).

3.2 Directional Constraint and Lyapunov-Style Descent

The prompt includes the directive “to 10.” We formalize this as a Directional Constraint Operator \mathcal{D} on any candidate sequence S. \mathcal{D} requires a strict Lyapunov-style descent condition on the distance to the target:

\forall i \ge 0,\quad d(s_{i+1}, \tau) < d(s_i, \tau) \quad \text{whenever } s_i \neq \tau. \tag{1}

This condition ensures the sequence is a strict contraction toward \tau at every step. It is both necessary and sufficient for S to be a geodesic terminating at \tau without redundant oscillatory steps.

Lemma 1 (Zero-step case). If s_0 = \tau, then the only sequence satisfying (1) is the trivial sequence S = (\tau). Any sequence of length |S| > 1 necessarily violates (1) at the first step, because d(s_1, \tau) \ge 1 > 0 = d(s_0, \tau).

Proof. If s_0 = \tau, then d(s_0,\tau)=0. The condition (1) demands d(s_1,\tau) < 0, which is impossible. Hence no step is allowed; the sequence must halt at s_0. \square

Application to the experimental sequences:

· For S_{\text{top}} = (11, 10): d(11,10)=1, d(10,10)=0 < 1. Satisfies (1).
· For S_{\text{stat}} = (11,12,\dots,20): d(12,10)=2, d(11,10)=1, and 2 \nless 1. Violates (1) at step i=0.

Thus, S_{\text{stat}} is not a valid counting sequence in the topological manifold. We term this a divergent manifold escape—the trajectory leaves the basin of attraction of the target before counting begins.

3.3 Statistical Mapping and the Cardinality Heuristic

In contrast, the statistical mapping interprets “10” as the cardinality operator k and “count to” as “generate a list of k consecutive numbers.” The default increment is +1. The objective becomes:

\min_S \big| |S| - k \big|,\quad s_{i+1} = s_i + 1.

With s_0 = 11 and k = 10, the minimal-length sequence satisfying the cardinality constraint is S_{\text{stat}} = (11, \dots, 20). This output perfectly fulfills the statistical interpretation while ignoring \tau.

3.4 Lexical Force Fields and Energy Bifurcation

Why does the model default to the statistical mapping? We model the token “10” as a singularity point in the latent semantic space exerting two competing forces:

· Positional force F_\tau: Pulls the sequence toward the integer coordinate 10. It derives from a deep, narrow well U_{\text{topo}}(s) = \alpha\, d(s,\tau)^2 (or similar), with minimum at \tau.
· Magnitude force F_k: Expands the sequence to length 10. This force arises from a broad, shallow well U_{\text{stat}}(s) centered on the forward-counting path. Its support is proportional to the frequency of “count to N” patterns in training data (millions of examples).

The total potential energy is U = U_{\text{topo}} + U_{\text{stat}}. Without an externally supplied reasoning work term W_{\text{reason}}, the model's selection of a sequence follows a Boltzmann-like distribution:

P(\text{path}) \propto \exp\bigl(-\beta \, \Delta U\bigr),

where \beta is an inverse temperature parameter and \Delta U is the effective energy barrier to that path. Because the statistical well has a much larger cross-sectional support in training data, the energy barrier to the forward path is effectively lower than the barrier to reversing direction toward \tau. The model falls into the F_k attractor basin and outputs S_{\text{stat}}. This is Potential Energy Minimization, inherently biased against counter-intuitive topological logic.

To escape this basin, the model must perform work W_{\text{reason}}. LLM4's internal trace “I need to go backwards” supplies this activation energy, elevating it out of the statistical minimum into the topological well. Figure 1 illustrates the dual-well landscape.

Figure 1: Lexical Potential Energy Landscape for Token “10”.
A one-dimensional reaction coordinate represents the sequence's intended final state. The broad, shallow well “Cardinality Attractor (k=10)” corresponds to the forward-counting manifold. The narrow, deep well “Coordinate Attractor (\tau=10)” lies at the geodesic target. The arrow W_{\text{reason}} indicates the activation energy from explicit System‑2 reasoning that lifts the agent from the statistical well into the topological well.

3.5 Categorical Formalization of the Reversal Curse

The Reversal Curse (Berglund et al., 2023) is the phenomenon that LLMs trained on “A is B” fail to deduce “B is A.” We provide a categorical model that explains this structurally.

Let \mathbf{OrdSet} be the category of finite ordered sets and order-preserving (monotone) maps. A forward-counting sequence from x to x+n can be seen as a morphism f: I \to \mathbb{Z} where I = \{0,1,\dots,n\} and f(i) = x+i. In the training data, such morphisms point forward and are never presented as invertible. Consequently, in the model's internal category \mathcal{C}, these counting morphisms are treated as monomorphisms—one-way arrows with no guarantee of an inverse.

However, the underlying integer structure with step \pm1 forms a groupoid: the successor function \text{succ}: n \mapsto n+1 has an inverse \text{pred}: n+1 \mapsto n. All basic step morphisms are isomorphisms. A reasoning-capable model like LLM4 implicitly operates in this groupoid, recognizing that the path from 11 to 10 is simply the inverse of the path from 10 to 11. It possesses an Inversion Functor (-)^{-1}: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C} that flips arrows.

LLM5 fails because its internal category lacks the Inversion Functor. The morphism 11 \to 20 has no meaningful inverse; the model cannot see “10” as a destination because its arrow space is not symmetric. This categorical absence is structural—no amount of scaling can create inverses if the basic building blocks are not defined as invertible.

Theorem (Categorical Inversion Failure). Let \mathcal{C} be the internal category of an LLM encoding counting sequences. If all base morphisms are non-invertible forward steps (monomorphisms in the Markov sense) and no inverse functor is defined, then for any target \tau < s_0, no morphism s_0 \to \tau exists in \mathcal{C}.

Proof. Morphisms in \mathcal{C} are composed of atomic step morphisms of the form n \to n+1 (since negative steps are not represented as invertible arrows). Compositions of such steps can only increase the integer value. Thus, a target \tau < s_0 is unreachable. \square

This directly yields the Reversal Curse: without an Inversion Functor, the model cannot reverse a known direction.

3.6 Stability Index with KL-Divergence Penalty

Within the SRI framework, the Stability Index \sigma quantifies an agent's ability to maintain coherent reasoning under increasing prompt entropy H(L). We refine \sigma to penalize mismatches between the model's internal reasoning trace T and its output actions.

Let the model emit a reasoning trace T from which we can infer a probability distribution P(D \mid T) over the intended step direction D \in \{-1,+1\}. The output sequence S yields an empirical action distribution Q(D). The Kullback-Leibler divergence D_{KL}(P \parallel Q) measures the inconsistency. The refined Stability Index is:

\sigma = \frac{1}{H(L)} \Bigl[ \mathbb{E}\bigl[P(\Phi_i \mid T)\bigr] - \beta\, D_{KL}\bigl(P(D \mid T) \parallel Q(D)\bigr) \Bigr], \tag{2}

where \beta \ge 0 is a sensitivity parameter, and P(\Phi_i \mid T) is the posterior over the chosen logical mapping \Phi_i (topological or statistical). When T and S are perfectly consistent, the KL term vanishes and \sigma reduces to the normalized expectation of correct mapping. When they conflict, the KL term grows, driving \sigma downward.

Hard Constraint Theorem (Hallucination Bound). Define a threshold \sigma_{\min}. If for a prompt L the agent produces output S with reasoning trace T such that

\sigma < \sigma_{\min},

then the agent is classified as Unstable for that prompt. In particular, if P(D=-1 \mid T) \approx 1 but Q(D=-1) = 0, then D_{KL} \to \infty (or a large finite value under smoothing), which forces \sigma \to 0 < \sigma_{\min}. Thus, a model that explicitly reasons backward but outputs forward is mathematically proven to be hallucinating relative to its own reasoning.

Proof. Rearranging (2) gives the condition. A detailed derivation is provided in Section 4.4. \square

For LLM5, which did not emit a visible trace, we may assume a uninformative prior P(D) = (0.5, 0.5). The KL divergence between this and Q = (0,1) is \log 2, a substantial penalty, indicating unacknowledged uncertainty and instability.

