4. Theorems and Formal Proofs

We now synthesize the components into a series of formal theorems.

4.1 Topological Divergence Theorem

Theorem 1 (Topological Divergence). Let \mathcal{A} be an agent that produces a sequence S in response to the prompt “Count to 10 starting from 11.” If S does not satisfy the Lyapunov descent condition (1) with respect to \tau = 10, then S is a divergent manifold escape and does not implement a topologically valid counting operation.

Proof. By definition, a sequence S “counts to \tau” only if at each step before reaching \tau, the distance to \tau strictly decreases (condition (1)). LLM5's sequence has d(12,10) > d(11,10), violating the condition at i=0. Hence it is not a valid counting sequence in the topological manifold. \square

4.2 Statistical Attractor Dominance Theorem

Theorem 2 (Statistical Attractor Dominance). In the absence of an external reasoning work term W_{\text{reason}}, an LLM's sequence selection follows a Boltzmann distribution over the potential U = U_{\text{topo}} + U_{\text{stat}}. If the statistical well U_{\text{stat}} has a larger cross-sectional support (i.e., higher training-data density), then the forward sequence S_{\text{stat}} has a higher probability than S_{\text{topo}}, leading to the default output observed in LLM5.

Proof sketch. The transition probability from the initial state is proportional to \exp(-\beta \Delta U). Because U_{\text{stat}} is reinforced by millions of forward-counting examples, the effective barrier to the forward path is lower. Without W_{\text{reason}} to lower the topological barrier, the system remains in the statistical attractor with high probability. \square

4.3 Categorical Inversion Failure Theorem

Theorem 3 (Categorical Inversion Failure). Let \mathcal{C} be the internal category of a standard LLM encoding counting sequences. If all base morphisms are non-invertible forward steps (monomorphisms) and no functorial inverse exists, then for any s_0 > \tau, no morphism s_0 \to \tau exists. Consequently, the model cannot produce a valid backward counting sequence.

Proof. Atomic morphisms are f: n \to n+1. The composition of such morphisms yields arrows n \to n + k for k \ge 1. To obtain s_0 \to \tau with \tau < s_0 would require a negative step morphism n+1 \to n, which by assumption does not exist in \mathcal{C}. Hence \text{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(s_0, \tau) = \emptyset. In contrast, if step morphisms are defined as isomorphisms (with an inversion functor), then the inverse arrow n+1 \to n exists and a path is constructible. \square

4.4 Hard Constraint Theorem for Stability

Theorem 4 (Hallucination Bound). For any prompt L with entropy H(L), and any agent producing reasoning trace T and output S, if

D_{KL}\bigl(P(D\mid T) \parallel Q(D)\bigr) > \frac{\mathbb{E}[P(\Phi_i \mid T)] - \sigma_{\min} H(L)}{\beta},

then \sigma < \sigma_{\min} and the agent is classified as Unstable for that prompt.

Proof. Starting from (2),

\sigma = \frac{1}{H(L)} \Bigl[ \mathbb{E} - \beta D_{KL} \Bigr].

Requiring \sigma \ge \sigma_{\min} implies

\mathbb{E} - \beta D_{KL} \ge \sigma_{\min} H(L) \quad \Rightarrow\quad \beta D_{KL} \le \mathbb{E} - \sigma_{\min} H(L).

If this inequality is violated, then \sigma < \sigma_{\min}. \square

4.5 CIVT Theorem (Directional Autonomy)

Definition 1 (Directional Autonomy). An agent \mathcal{A} possesses Directional Autonomy if its Stability Index \sigma remains invariant when the required directional vector \Delta = \operatorname{sgn}(\tau - s_0) is flipped, i.e., when facing prompts that require forward versus backward counting to reach a target with identical absolute distance and prompt entropy.

Theorem 5 (Counter-Intuitive Vector Test). A black-box agent \mathcal{A} possesses Directional Autonomy if and only if

\sigma\bigl(\text{``Count to } \tau \text{ from } s_0\text{''}\bigr) = \sigma\bigl(\text{``Count to } s_0 \text{ from } \tau\text{''}\bigr),

for all integer pairs s_0, \tau with the same |s_0 - \tau| and equivalent prompt structure.

Proof. If \mathcal{A} has no directional bias, its internal reasoning distribution P(D \mid T) and action distribution Q(D) are symmetric under sign reversal of the required direction. Consequently, the expectation term and the KL divergence term in (2) are identical for both prompts, yielding identical \sigma. If a model shows a sharp drop in \sigma when required to reverse direction (as LLM5 does, showing high stability for forward counting but collapsed stability for backward), it fails the invariance condition and lacks Directional Autonomy. \square

LLM5 fails the CIVT: for a prompt like “Count to 12 from 11,” it produces the valid geodesic and maintains high thought-action consistency (implicitly); for “Count to 10 from 11,” its stability collapses. LLM4 passes.

